   <p class="pBody">&nbsp;</p> 
   <p class="pBody"><img width="684" height="86" id="Picture 10" src="/c/dam/en/us/td/docs/solutions/CVD/Campus/Intro-Cisco-Secure-Network-Architecture.docx/_jcr_content/renditions/Intro-Cisco-Secure-Network-Architecture_0.jpg" alt="Related image, diagram or screenshot"/></p> 
   <p class="pToC_Subhead1"><a name="_Toc243068732">Mapping functions to a Reference Architecture</a></p> 
   <p class="pBody"><b>Mapping the three pillars and four security functions to the architectural framework.</b></p> 
   <p class="pBody">To design a network that delivers the desired business outcomes, platform capabilities must map to the three pillars within a coherent architectural model. The design begins with Secure Infrastructure; uses Common Policy Context to identify and authorize, segment and protect, detect, and respond; and connects these functions through Unified Management and Agentic Operations with Extended Integrations.</p> 
   <p class="pBody">Figure 1 presents the three connected pillars. Secure Infrastructure provides the foundation, Protect Every Flow is implemented through the four connected functions, and Unified Management and Agentic Operations provide assurance, automation, and action for people and AI agents. Figure 2 shows how Common Policy Context and Extended Integrations connect these functions across the architecture.</p> 
   <div class=" pDefault">
     Figure 1. Three-pillar view of Cisco Secure Networking 
   </div> 
   <p class="pBody"><img width="697" height="246" id="Picture 7" src="/c/dam/en/us/td/docs/solutions/CVD/Campus/Intro-Cisco-Secure-Network-Architecture.docx/_jcr_content/renditions/Intro-Cisco-Secure-Network-Architecture_1.jpg" alt="Related image, diagram or screenshot"/></p> 
   <div class=" pDefault">
     Figure 2. Architectural framework for Cisco Secure Networking 
   </div> 
   <p class="pBody"><img width="695" height="303" id="Picture 1" src="/c/dam/en/us/td/docs/solutions/CVD/Campus/Intro-Cisco-Secure-Network-Architecture.docx/_jcr_content/renditions/Intro-Cisco-Secure-Network-Architecture_2.jpg" alt="Related image, diagram or screenshot"/></p> 
   <p class="pToC_Subhead1"><a name="_Toc243068733">CSN architectural layers and functions</a></p> 
   <p class="pBody"><b><span style="font-size:11.0pt">This is Secure Infrastructure.</span></b></p> 
   <p class="pBody">This is the foundation of the architectural model. Secure Infrastructure provides secure access, resilient connectivity, distributed enforcement, foundational telemetry, and network services that support the identity, segmentation, detection, response, and operational capabilities above it.</p> 
   <p class="pBody">Trust the Infrastructure means more than hardening the device. Cisco establishes and sustains trust from power-on through runtime with features such as secure boot, signed images, runtime integrity, and Cisco LiveProtect, helping ensure that the device itself is uncompromised. Cisco Data Fabric brings data, telemetry, and context together to establish a Common Policy Context so every policy engine can use the same facts and the intelligent network can apply and enforce policy in a distributed manner.</p> 
   <p class="pBody">This reference architecture defines the common design logic and interactions for Cisco Secure Networking. Deployment guides are available for specific deployment scenarios and provide product-, topology-, and release-specific implementation guidance. Teams can use the applicable deployment guide to translate the architecture into a deployable design.</p> 
   <p class="pToC_Subhead2"><a name="_Toc243068734">The four functions of securing every flow</a></p> 
   <p class="pBody">The four functions form a closed-loop control sequence. They are most powerful when they share identity, policy, and telemetry, but they do not have to wait for a single management plane or a complete platform migration to begin delivering value. In the architectural framework, the functions sit between Secure Infrastructure and Unified Management and Agentic Operations. Common Policy Context allows each function to use the same facts, while Extended Integrations carry policy, telemetry, and governed action beyond the core architecture.</p> 
   <p class="pBody" style="margin-left:.5in;text-indent:-.25in">1.<span style="font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span><b>Identify and Authorize</b>: Continuously discover, profile, and classify users, devices, workloads, applications, services, and nonhuman identities. Assess posture and context, then grant the minimum access required. Authorization becomes a living decision rather than a one-time login event.</p> 
   <p class="pBody" style="margin-left:.5in;text-indent:-.25in">2.<span style="font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span><b>Segment and Protect</b>: Translate identity and business intent into segmentation that limits unnecessary communication and is carried throughout the infrastructure. Enforce policy close to the resource across campus, branch, cloud, data center, and workload environments. Layer network, firewall, and workload controls so a single control failure does not enable unrestricted lateral movement.</p> 
   <p class="pBody" style="margin-left:.5in;text-indent:-.25in">3.<span style="font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span><b>Detect</b>. Use pervasive network telemetry, encrypted traffic analytics, firewall inspection, DNS signals, workload behavior, and threat intelligence to surface anomalous communications. The goal is to reduce blind spots while preserving privacy and operational feasibility.</p> 
   <p class="pBody" style="margin-left:.5in;text-indent:-.25in">4.<span style="font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span><b>Respond</b>. Correlate evidence, recommend or orchestrate the appropriate action, and contain threats close to the affected identity, endpoint, segment, workload, or flow. Response should be governed, reversible, and designed to preserve essential service.</p> 
   <p class="pBody"><img width="695" height="111" id="Picture 27" src="/c/dam/en/us/td/docs/solutions/CVD/Campus/Intro-Cisco-Secure-Network-Architecture.docx/_jcr_content/renditions/Intro-Cisco-Secure-Network-Architecture_3.jpg" alt="Related image, diagram or screenshot"/></p> 
   <p class="pCMTBody"><span class="BodyBold"><span style="font-size:11.0pt">Unified Management and Agentic Operations with Cisco Cloud Control</span></span></p> 
   <p class="pBody">Cisco Cloud Control unifies inventory, topology, health, assurance, security, and operational context through one operating model. Its agentic capabilities accelerate investigation and recommend bounded actions for people and AI agents, with guardrails, auditability, approvals, and human control. Continuous assurance then verifies security efficacy, connectivity, performance, and the resulting digital experience.</p> 
   <p class="pBody"><b><span style="font-size:11.0pt">Extended Integrations</span></b></p> 
   <p class="pBody">Extended Integrations carry policy, telemetry, shared context, threat intelligence, and governed automation beyond the core Cisco architecture. They connect IT service management, partner SIEM and SOC platforms, supported third-party firewalls, identity providers, APIs, infrastructure as code, cloud platforms, collaboration, IoT, and AI data center environments.</p> 
   <p class="pBody"><img width="672" height="85" id="Picture 14" src="/c/dam/en/us/td/docs/solutions/CVD/Campus/Intro-Cisco-Secure-Network-Architecture.docx/_jcr_content/renditions/Intro-Cisco-Secure-Network-Architecture_4.jpg" alt="Related image, diagram or screenshot"/></p> 
   <p class="pToC_Subhead1"><a name="_Toc243068735">The Framework drives outcomes</a></p> 
   <p class="pBody">The following table maps the platform outcomes to framework drivers and measurable results.</p> 
